  "response_text": "Always choosing the highest-priority task is a bad idea because it leads to starvation of lower-priority tasks. This means that if a high-priority task never yields or if new high-priority tasks keep arriving, lower-priority threads may never get a chance to run. This violates fairness and can break programs that depend on background or maintenance tasks (e.g., logging, communications) which might typically be assigned a lower priority, but are still essential. It also makes it difficult to predict the system behaviour since the lower priority tasks will keep getting pushed back, which can make real-time behavior unreliable.\\n",
